x-the-underlying-quotient-graph-does-not-determine-the-group"]},{"id":"thm-characters-of-direct-sums-tensor-products-and-duals","statement":"For every g in G: chi_{V direct-sum W}(g)=chi_V(g)+chi_W(g), chi_{V tensor W}(g)=chi_V(g)chi_W(g), and chi_{V^*}(g)=overline(chi_V(g)).","used_by":["thm-character-inner-product-computes-intertwiner-dimension","cor-multiplicity-of-an-irreducible-summand-is-a-character-inner-product","cor-a-complex-character-is-irreducible-iff-its-self-inner-product-is-one","cor-the-regular-character-gives-the-sum-of-squares-formula","ex-the-standard-representation-of-s-n-has-character-fixed-points-minus-one","ex-decomposing-the-square-of-the-two-dimensional-s-three-character","ex-the-character-table-of-s-four-and-its-normal-subgroups"]},{"id":"thm-first-orthogonality-relation-for-irreducible-complex-characters","statement":"Let chi_1,...,chi_r be the irreducible complex characters of G. Then <chi_i,chi_j>=delta_ij for 1<=i,j<=r.","used_by":["thm-irreducible-complex-characters-form-an-orthonormal-basis-of-the-class-functions","cor-multiplicity-of-an-irreducible-summand-is-a-character-inner-product","ex-the-character-table-of-a-finite-cyclic-group-over-c","ex-the-character-table-of-s-three","ex-the-character-table-of-a-four","ex-the-character-table-of-q-eight","ex-the-character-table-of-dih-c-four"]},{"id":"thm-fundamental-group-acts-on-its-bass-serre-tree","statement":"Gamma acts on its Bass-Serre tree by left multiplication on cosets, the action is without inversions, the quotient graph is X, and stabilizers are gamma G_v gamma^{-1} and gamma G_e gamma^{-1}.","used_by":["thm-fundamental-groups-from-different-maximal-trees-are-isomorphic","cor-fundamental-group-of-a-graph-with-trivial-groups-is-free","thm-kurosh-subgroup-theorem","fs-vertex-stabilizers-are-literally-the-chosen-vertex-groups-without-conjugacy","fs-every-tree-action-is-a-free-action","ex-bass-serre-tree-of-a-free-product","ex-graph-of-finite-groups-giving-a-virtually-free-group"]},{"id":"thm-lying-over","statement":"Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let f:A->B be an integral ring map, and let p in Spec(A) with ker f subseteq p. Then there exists a prime ideal q in Spec(B) such that f^{-1}(q)=p.","used_by":["thm-going-up","cor-dimension-preserved-by-integral-extensions","ex-lying-over-by-quotient-and-localisation"]}],"published_dependencies":[{"id":"prop-equality-of-words-in-a-presentation","used_for":"Used to identify triviality in a presented group with membership in the normal closure of the relators, both for recursive enumerability and for Dehn-function and Tietze arguments.","opened":true},{"id":"prop-normal-closure-is-products-of-conjugates","used_for":"Used to enumerate relator consequences and to define algebraic relator area by finite products of conjugates.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-tietze-transformations-connect-finite-presentations","used_for":"Used to reduce invariance of the word problem under changing generators/presentations to checking single Tietze moves.","opened":true},{"id":"def-alphabet-words-and-reduction","used_for":"Used for the free-reduction algorithm and the free-group word-problem criterion.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-normal-form-for-the-word-quotient-model","used_for":"Used to identify the unique reduced representative of the identity class in the free-group word-problem theorem.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-normal-form-for-free-products-with-amalgamation","used_for":"Used for the solvable-word-problem theorem for amalgams and for the graph-of-groups normal-form development.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-hnn-normal-form-theorem","used_for":"Used in the graph-of-groups normal-form theorem and the HNN special-case page items.","opened":true},{"id":"def-oriented-graph-with-edge-reversal","used_for":"Used to fix the orientation and reversal convention underlying graphs of groups and quotient graphs.","opened":true},{"id":"def-quotient-graph-of-an-action-without-inversions","used_for":"Used in the quotient graph-of-groups construction and in the free-action example.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-prime-spectrum-of-a-localisation-bijection","used_for":"Used repeatedly in lying over, incomparability, going down, and the height-comparison corollary.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-integrality-and-finite-module-equivalences","used_for":"Used in transitivity of integrality, normality-local arguments, and the k[X^2] example.","opened":true},{"id":"cor-integral-elements-form-a-subring","used_for":"Used to show integral closures are subrings and in the explicit integral-elements example.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-proper-ideal-contained-in-maximal-ideal","used_for":"Used in lying-over and normality-local proofs when maximal ideals are needed from proper ideals.","opened":true},{"id":"def-finite-dimensional-representation-of-a-group-over-a-field","used_for":"Used to define characters, tensor products, duals, fixed subspaces, and the standing representation-theory scope.","opened":true},{"id":"def-inner-product-space","used_for":"Used to pin down the linear-first Hermitian convention imported by the class-function inner product.","opened":true},{"id":"cor-finite-dimensional-representations-are-completely-reducible-when-char-k-does-not-divide-group-order","used_for":"Used for complete reducibility throughout the character-theory page, especially multiplicity and character-determination arguments.","opened":true},{"id":"cor-schurs-lemma-for-irreducible-representations","used_for":"Used in the first orthogonality relation and related irreducibility arguments.","opened":true},{"id":"cor-endomorphisms-of-an-irreducible-over-an-algebraically-closed-field-are-scalars","used_for":"Used in the first orthogonality relation and in the abelian/splitting-field character arguments.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-number-of-irreducible-representations-equals-the-number-of-conjugacy-classes-when-k-is-algebraically-closed-and-char-k-does-not-divide-group-order","used_for":"Used to count irreducibles, prove the character-basis theorem, and close the abelian-degree-one criterion.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-degree-one-representations-are-exactly-homomorphisms-to-k-times-and-form-an-abelian-group","used_for":"Used in the cyclic, A4, Q8, and dihedral character-table examples to construct and classify degree-one rows.","opened":true}],"concerns":[{"id":"lem-trivial-words-in-a-recursively-presented-group-are-recursively-enumerable","concern":"Step 2.1 accepts only when the freely reduced relator product is literally the input word w. For a nonreduced trivial word, the search can miss a valid certificate because every enumerated certificate is compared to the reduced form rather than to w up to free equivalence.","severity":"would-be-fatal"},{"id":"def-reduced-graph-of-groups-word","concern":"The last sentence says tree edges are omitted from graph-of-groups words because they are trivial in pi_1(G,T). As written that leaves no syntax for ordinary positive-length words in an amalgamated-product case where moving between two vertex-group coefficients still requires the tree edge in the normal form.","severity":"would-be-fatal"},{"id":"thm-normal-form-for-fundamental-groups-of-graphs-of-groups","concern":"Step 1.2 says that if a chosen geometric edge e lies in the maximal subtree T, then removing e splits X into connected components. That is false when X has extra non-tree edges closing a cycle; deleting a tree edge from a spanning tree need not disconnect the whole graph.","severity":"would-be-fatal"},{"id":"rem-standing-hypotheses-for-ordinary-character-theory","concern":"The remark says every item on the page works with finite G, k=C, and finite-dimensional representations, but prop-representations-with-kernel-containing-a-normal-subgroup-factor-through-the-quotient is explicitly stated for an arbitrary group and field.","severity":"would-be-fatal"},{"id":"def-character-of-a-complex-representation","concern":"The displayed definition introduces a representation rho:G->GL(V) but defines chi_V(g)=tr(rho_V(g)). The symbol rho_V is never introduced here, so the defining formula has a notation mismatch at the point of definition.","severity":"presentation"},{"id":"def-irreducible-complex-character","concern":"The well-definedness paragraph says equivalence preserves irreducibility, but gives no citation or local argument transporting invariant subspaces through an invertible intertwiner.","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"},{"id":"def-character-table-of-a-finite-group","concern":"The definition uses the claim that each irreducible character is constant on conjugacy classes before that property has been proved on the page or cited from an earlier item.","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"},{"id":"def-tensor-product-of-complex-representations","concern":"The well-definedness paragraph uses that elementary tensors span V tensor_C W and that bases of V and W give the product basis of the tensor product, but neither point is cited locally.","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"},{"id":"def-dual-complex-representation","concern":"The final sentence concludes that the dual is a representation of the same degree as rho, but the cited dependencies define V* and finite-dimensional representations without supplying dim(V*)=dim(V).","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"},{"id":"thm-integrality-commutes-with-localisation","concern":"In step 2.1, from a in overline(A) the proof cites step 1.1 to conclude x=a/s is integral over S^{-1}A. Step 1.1 only gives integrality of a/1 after localisation; closure under multiplication by the base element 1/s is left unstated.","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"}],"alerts":[],"seams_checked":[]}